---
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import RustIngestProjectVisual from "../../components/RustIngestProjectVisual.astro";
import PortfolioLayout from "../../layouts/PortfolioLayout.astro";
import { featuredProjects } from "../../data/portfolio";
import { withBase } from "../../utils/paths";

const project = featuredProjects.find((item) => item.href === "/projects/rust-ingest");
---

<PortfolioLayout title="Htet Lin Maung | Rust Payment Event Pipeline" currentPath="/projects">
	<section class="page-banner">
		<div class="container project-detail-hero">
			<div class="project-detail-copy">
				<p class="eyebrow">Project Detail</p>
				<h1>Rust Payment Event Pipeline</h1>
				<p class="project-repository-name">
					<Icon name="github" />
					<span>Open-source repository</span>
					<code>rust-ingest</code>
				</p>
				<p>
					A payment notification pipeline built with Rust on AWS. It handles high volumes of
					incoming payment events without dropping data or running up a large cloud bill.
				</p>
				<p class="project-detail-note">
					This is a prototype. It is not connected to any real payment provider or production
					system.
				</p>

				<ul class="chip-list project-detail-stack">
					<li>Rust</li>
					<li>Amazon API Gateway</li>
					<li>AWS Lambda</li>
					<li>Amazon SQS</li>
					<li>DynamoDB</li>
					<li>AWS CDK</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="project-detail-actions">
					<a class="button primary" href={project?.sourceHref} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
						View Source
						<Icon name="github" />
					</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href={withBase("/projects")}>
						Back to Projects
						<Icon name="arrow" />
					</a>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-visual">
				<RustIngestProjectVisual />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="project-detail-story">
				<div class="project-detail-story-intro">
					<p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
					<h2 class="section-title">A queue-first design for reliability and cost control.</h2>
					<p>
						The system keeps the public request path fast while durable background processing
						handles persistence.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="project-detail-story-copy">
					<article class="project-detail-narrative">
						<span>01</span>
						<h3>The problem</h3>
					<p>
						When a customer pays online, the payment provider sends a notification to the
						merchant&apos;s server. That works fine when traffic is low. Now imagine Black Friday:
						thousands of payments happening every second.
					</p>
					<p>
						Events get dropped if the server is busy saving to a database when the next
						notification arrives, and it gets expensive when teams keep large servers running
						around the clock just to survive traffic spikes.
					</p>
					</article>

					<article class="project-detail-narrative">
						<span>02</span>
						<h3>The approach</h3>
						<p>
							The pipeline is split into two parts connected by a queue. The first part accepts
							incoming notifications as fast as possible and puts them on the queue. It never
							touches the database directly, so a slow database never slows it down.
						</p>
						<p>
							The second part reads from the queue and saves each notification to the database.
							If one fails, only that one is retried. If a message keeps failing, it can move to a
							dead-letter queue instead of blocking the main queue.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section project-detail-architecture-section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="section-head project-detail-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
				<h2 class="section-title">Receiver, queue, processor, storage.</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-architecture">
				<div class="project-detail-architecture-card">
					<picture>
						<source
							media="(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
							srcset={withBase("/projects/rust-ingest-architecture-poster.png")}
						/>
						<img
							class="project-detail-architecture-image"
							src={withBase("/projects/rust-ingest-architecture.gif")}
							width="1100"
							height="600"
							loading="lazy"
							decoding="async"
							alt="Animated rust-ingest architecture showing payment events flowing through API Gateway, Rust Lambda functions, SQS, DynamoDB, and a dead-letter queue"
						/>
					</picture>
				</div>

				<div class="project-detail-architecture-notes">
					<article>
						<span>01</span>
						<h3>Fast acceptance path</h3>
						<p>
							The first part accepts incoming notifications as fast as possible and puts them on
							the queue. It just acknowledges the request and moves on.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article>
						<span>02</span>
						<h3>Retry-safe processing</h3>
						<p>
							The queue holds notifications safely. Even if thousands arrive at once, nothing is
							lost. Failed messages retry independently, and repeated failures can be inspected
							separately instead of getting stuck in an infinite retry loop.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container project-detail-section">
			<div class="section-head project-detail-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Engineering Focus</p>
				<h2 class="section-title">Designed for bursts, failure, and simple operations.</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-principles">
				<article>
					<h3>Rust on Lambda</h3>
					<p>
						Low memory usage and fast startup make Rust a good fit for Lambda when you want a
						serverless ingest path to stay efficient under bursty workloads.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article>
					<h3>SQS decoupling</h3>
					<p>
						Amazon SQS decouples receiving from processing. Messages are held safely during
						traffic spikes and retried automatically on failure.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article>
					<h3>DynamoDB storage</h3>
					<p>
						Serverless NoSQL with pay-per-request billing and built-in TTL support makes
						DynamoDB a practical fit for expiring old payment records without managing a cluster.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-build">
				<div class="project-detail-build-heading">
					<p class="eyebrow">Code Structure</p>
					<h2>Clear boundaries from request to infrastructure.</h2>
				</div>

				<div class="project-detail-build-list">
					<article>
						<span>01</span>
						<h3>Receiver path</h3>
						<p>
							<code>bin/receiver.rs</code> is a thin HTTP adapter while
							<code>services/enqueue.rs</code> contains the receiver business logic that
							publishes events.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article>
						<span>02</span>
						<h3>Processor path</h3>
						<p>
							<code>bin/processor.rs</code> is a thin SQS adapter while
							<code>services/persist.rs</code> owns the processor business logic for saving events.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article>
						<span>03</span>
						<h3>AWS wrappers</h3>
						<p>
							<code>aws/queue.rs</code> and <code>aws/dynamodb.rs</code> keep service integration
							separate from domain logic for easier testing and clearer boundaries.
						</p>
					</article>

					<article>
						<span>04</span>
						<h3>Infrastructure as code</h3>
						<p>
							The infrastructure is defined in TypeScript CDK, making the queues, Lambdas, API
							Gateway, and DynamoDB setup easier to review and test than raw CloudFormation.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="project-detail-closing">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">Explore the implementation</p>
					<h2>Read the code, infrastructure, and deployment notes.</h2>
				</div>
				<a class="button primary" href={project?.sourceHref} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
					Open GitHub
					<Icon name="github" />
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</PortfolioLayout>

<style>
	.project-repository-name {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.45rem;
		margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
		font-family: "Manrope", sans-serif;
		font-size: 0.72rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: #6a7893;
	}

	.project-repository-name :global(.icon) {
		width: 1rem;
		height: 1rem;
		color: #24292f;
	}

	.project-repository-name code {
		padding: 0.18rem 0.38rem;
		border-radius: 0.35rem;
		background: #edf3ff;
		font-size: 0.7rem;
		color: #2c5ecc;
	}
</style>
